category: environment

entry spent-fuel
term: Spent Nuclear Fuel
difficulty: intermediate
related_terms: radioactive-waste
related_terms: fuel-rods
related_terms: low-level-waste
related_terms: plutonium-239
related_terms: reactor-core
quick_facts: Composition: ~93% uranium · ~5% fission products · ~1% plutonium
quick_facts: Water pools first (≥5 years), then sealed dry casks
quick_facts: Casks survive crashes, fires, and floods by design
quick_facts: ~95% of its possible energy is still unused — recycling exists
quick_facts: Finland's Onkalo is the first permanent deep repository

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Storage and Disposal of Spent Nuclear Fuel","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/nuclear-wastes.aspx"}

definition:
    Fuel assemblies removed from a reactor after roughly 4–6 years, when fission products build up and efficiency drops. Far from empty, an assembly still holds about 93% uranium-238, ~1% plutonium, and ~5% split fragments — leaving most of its original energy unburned. Fresh out of the core it's fiercely radioactive and heat-generating, so it spends at least five years underwater, then decades in ventilated dry casks while nations debate deep repositories or recycling.

debunking_snippet:
    “Spent fuel is useless garbage.” It's mostly untouched uranium plus freshly made plutonium — countries like France reprocess it into new fuel rods rather than burying it.
